/-! # Effective generators of the Picard group of a flag variety

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Lemma 18.21 and its proof, printed p.392;
Proposition 13.52 and the proof of Theorem 17.65.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-flag-effective-picard-generators)
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory Limits MonoidalCategory CartesianMonoidalCategory
open scoped MonObj

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The Picard group of a Borel quotient has finitely many invertible generators,
each admitting a nonzero global section. The right-coset presheaf is the coequalizer
of `(g,b) ↦ g` and `(g,b) ↦ gb`; `ηQ` exhibits its fppf sheafification as represented by Q.
The existing Picard group uses multiplicative notation: the product of integer powers
below is precisely an integer linear combination for the tensor-product group law.
The empty family is allowed.

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Lemma 18.21 and proof, printed p.392.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-flag-effective-picard-generators)

Proof, following the six steps of the node:
1. By lem-sfb-borel-quotient-affine-cell, Q is regular, integral, Noetherian and
   separated, with a dense open U isomorphic over F to affine n-space.
2. By lem-sc-noetherian-closed-subset-finite-components, the boundary has finitely
   many irreducible components. Its codimension-one components E_i are exactly the
   prime divisors in the boundary: strict containment in a larger boundary component
   would give a chain of length two below Q, contradicting codimension one.
3. The local rings of Q are UFDs by
   lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization with no chosen parameters.
   By lem-trf-ufd-height-one, each E_i has a locally principal ideal generated by a
   nonzerodivisor. Off E_i use the generator 1. The scheme clause of
   lem-sfb-stalk-nonzerodivisor-cartier-spreading spreads these stalk equations,
   making E_i an effective Cartier divisor.
4. Use lem-sfb-regular-space-divisor-line-dictionary to form L_i = O_Q(E_i).
   Locally it is f_i⁻¹ O_Q. Its canonical rational section 1 is regular, since
   1 = f_i · f_i⁻¹, and these sections glue. On U it is a generator, hence nonzero.
5. Any invertible M is trivial on U by lem-sfb-affine-space-invertible-trivial.
   Its generator on U gives a nonzero rational section. The divisor-line dictionary
   gives M isomorphic to O_Q(D); restriction shows that D is supported on the
   boundary. Step 2 therefore writes D as an integer sum of the E_i.
6. On common local equation charts, multiplication identifies O_Q(Σ a_i E_i)
   with the tensor product of the L_i to integer powers, using duals for negative
   exponents: both are (∏ f_i^a_i)⁻¹ O_Q. The identifications agree inside the
   rational-function field and glue. Thus every Picard class is the displayed
   product. If r = 0 the empty product is 1 and every invertible module is trivial. -/
theorem flag_effective_picard_generators
    {F : Type u} [Field F] [IsAlgClosed F]
    (G B : Over (Spec (.of F))) [GrpObj G] [GrpObj B]
    [Smooth G.hom] [ConnectedSpace G.left] [IsAffine G.left]
    [LocallyOfFiniteType G.hom] [QuasiCompact G.hom]
    (i : B ⟶ G) (hB : IsBorelGroupScheme i)
    (Q : Over (Spec (.of F)))
    (ηQ : coequalizer (yoneda.map (fst G B))
      (yoneda.map (lift (fst G B) (snd G B ≫ i) ≫ μ[G])) ⟶ yoneda.obj Q)
    (hηQ : ∀ Z : Sheaf (Scheme.fppfTopology.over (Spec (.of F))) (Type u),
      Function.Bijective (fun f : yoneda.obj Q ⟶ Z.obj ↦ ηQ ≫ f)) :
    ∃ (r : ℕ) (L : Fin r → Q.left.Modules) (hL : ∀ j, (L j).IsInvertible),
      (∀ j, ∃ s : Γ(L j, ⊤), s ≠ 0) ∧
      ∀ M : Q.left.Pic, ∃ a : Fin r → ℤ,
        M = ∏ j, (@Scheme.Pic.ofIsInvertible Q.left (L j) (hL j)) ^ (a j) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
